 is on May 1st; Y can be C, D, E, F excluding X).\n\nSo, similar to Case 2:\n\nMay 1st: B and X (4 choices for X).\n\nMay 3rd: A and Y (3 choices for Y from remaining leaders).\n\nThen, May 2nd is the remaining two leaders.\n\nTherefore, number of arrangements: 4 \u00d7 3 = 12.\n\nCase 4: A is on May 3rd, B is on May 2nd.\n\nSo, May 3rd has A and someone else (Y), and May 2nd has B and someone else (X). Also, May 1st has the remaining two leaders.\n\nSo, let's see:\n\nLeaders available: A, B, C, D, E, F.\n\n- May 3rd: A and Y (Y \u2260 B).\n\n- May 2nd: B and X (X \u2260 A).\n\nSo, X can be any of C, D, E, F.\n\nSimilarly, Y can be any of C, D, E, F excluding X.\n\nTherefore, number of ways:\n\nChoose X (partner for B on May 2nd): 4 choices (C, D, E, F).\n\nChoose Y (partner for A on May 3rd): 3 choices (remaining after X is chosen).\n\nThen, May 1st is the remaining two leaders.\n\nSo, total arrangements: 4 \u00d7 3 = 12.\n\nWait, but hold on. In this case, A is on May 3rd and B is on May 2nd. So, similar to Cases 2 and 3, the count is 4 \u00d7 3 = 12.\n\nTherefore, summing up all four cases:\n\nCase 1: 6\n\nCase 2: 12\n\nCase 3: 12\n\nCase 4: 12\n\nTotal = 6 + 12 + 12 + 12 = 42.\n\nThis matches the previous result from inclusion-exclusion: 90 total - 48 invalid = 42 valid.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 42.\n\nBut let me verify once more to be sure.\n\nAlternative verification: Let's compute the number of valid schedules by considering the possible assignments for A and B, then the rest.\n\nSince A can only be on May 1st or 3rd, and B can only be on May 1st or 2nd.\n\nSo, possible assignments for A and B:\n\n1. A on May 1st, B on May 1st.\n\n2. A on May 1st, B on May 2nd.\n\n3. A on May 3rd, B on May 1st.\n\n4. A on May 3rd, B on May 2nd.\n\nThese four cases, as before.\n\nFor each case, compute the number of ways:\n\nCase 1: A and B on May 1st.\n\nThen, May 1st is A and B. The other days: May 2nd and 3rd need 2 leaders each from C, D, E, F. Number of ways: C(4,2) \u