uct divisible by 3.\n\nSo, suppose for contradiction that none of the ai is divisible by 3. Then each ai is either 1 or 2 mod3, so each ai\u00b3 is either 1 or 8 mod9.\n\nTherefore, the sum S = a1\u00b3 + a2\u00b3 + a3\u00b3 + a4\u00b3 + a5\u00b3 \u2261 sum of five terms each of which is 1 or 8 mod9.\n\nLet\u2019s denote that each term is either 1 or 8. Let\u2019s let x be the number of terms that are 1 mod9, and y be the number of terms that are 8 mod9. Then x + y =5, and the sum S \u2261 x*1 + y*8 mod9.\n\nWe are given that S \u22610 mod9. So, x +8y \u22610 mod9.\n\nBut x + y =5 \u21d2 x=5\u2212y. Substitute into the equation:\n\n(5\u2212y) +8y \u22610 mod9 \u21d25 +7y \u22610 mod9.\n\nSo, 7y \u2261\u22125 mod9. Since \u22125 mod9 is 4, so 7y\u22614 mod9.\n\nWe can solve for y. Multiply both sides by the inverse of 7 mod9. 7*4=28\u22611 mod9, so inverse of 7 is 4.\n\nThus, y \u22614*4=16\u22617 mod9.\n\nBut y is the number of terms which are 8 mod9, so y must be between 0 and5. So y=7 is impossible. Therefore, no solution exists where none of the ai is divisible by3.\n\nTherefore, our initial assumption that none of the ai is divisible by3 leads to a contradiction. Hence, at least one ai must be divisible by3, so the product a1a2a3a4a5 is divisible by3.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let's go through the steps.\n\nIf all ai \u22611 or 2 mod3, then each ai\u00b3 \u22611 or8 mod9. Then the sum is x +8y with x+y=5. Then x +8y \u22610 mod9. Then substituting x=5\u2212y, get 5 +7y \u22610 mod9. Then 7y\u2261-5\u22614 mod9. Multiply both sides by 4: 28y\u226116 mod9 \u21d21y\u22617 mod9. So y\u22617 mod9. But y is between 0 and5, so impossible. Therefore, no solution exists. Hence, our assumption that all ai are not divisible by3 is false. Therefore, at least one ai must be divisible by3. Hence, 3 divides the product. Therefore, proved.\n\nYes, that seems to work. So the key idea is that if none of the ai's were divisible by3, then their cubes modulo9 would be 1 or8,