n\nTherefore, by Chinese Remainder Theorem, 2^341 \u22612 mod11 and 2 mod31. The number congruent to 2 mod11 and 2 mod31 is 2 mod341. Therefore, 2^341 \u22612 mod341. Therefore, 2^341 -1 \u22611 mod341. Therefore, 341 does not divide 2^341 -1. Wait, so 341 is not a solution? But I thought it was a pseudoprime. Hmm. Maybe I confused something. Let me check.\n\nWait, a pseudoprime to base a is a composite number n such that a^(n-1) \u22611 modn. So 341 is a pseudoprime to base 2 if 2^340 \u22611 mod341. Let's check. 2^10=1024\u22611 mod11 (since 1024-1=1023=11*93). So 2^10\u22611 mod11. Therefore, 2^340=(2^10)^34\u22611^34=1 mod11.\n\nFor mod31: 2^5=32\u22611 mod31, so 2^5\u22611 mod31. Then 2^340=(2^5)^68\u22611^68=1 mod31. Therefore, 2^340\u22611 mod31. Therefore, by Chinese Remainder Theorem, 2^340\u22611 mod341. So 2^340\u22611 mod341, which means 2^341\u22612 mod341. Therefore, 341 is a pseudoprime to base 2 because 2^340\u22611 mod341. But 2^341 -1 would be 1 mod341, so 341 does not divide 2^341 -1. Therefore, 341 is not a solution. So my initial thought was wrong. Maybe there are other composite numbers?\n\nWait, let's check n=341. Since 2^340 \u22611 mod341, so 2^341 \u22612 mod341, so 2^341 -1 \u22611 mod341. So 341 does not divide 2^341 -1. So n=341 is out.\n\nWait, maybe n=1 is the only solution? But that seems unlikely. Let me check n=3. Wait, we saw n=3 doesn't work. How about n=1? Yes. Let's check n=0, but n must be positive integer, so n=1 is the only one so far.\n\nWait, but maybe there's another number. Let me think. Suppose n is an odd number. Since if n is even, then 2^n -1 is odd, so even n cannot divide an odd number unless n=1, which is even? Wait, n=1 is odd. Wait, if n is even, then n is at least 2. But 2^n -1 is odd, so an even number can't divide an odd number. Therefore, all even n>1 cannot be solutions. Therefore, only odd n need to be considered.\n\nSo, n must be odd. So n is odd, n\u22651. So n=1,3,5,7,9,... We saw n=1 works. Let me check