s both \u03c6(n) and n. So, ord_n(2) divides gcd(n,\u03c6(n)).\n\nIf n is 1, then it works. For n>1, since n is composite (primes don't work as shown before), we need that ord_n(2) divides n. But ord_n(2) also divides \u03c6(n). Therefore, ord_n(2) divides gcd(n, \u03c6(n)).\n\nBut for n composite, \u03c6(n) <n (for n>2), but gcd(n, \u03c6(n)) could be something. For example, let's take n=341. \u03c6(341)=\u03c6(11*31)=10*30=300. gcd(341,300)=1. So ord_341(2) divides 1, which would mean ord_341(2)=1, but 2^1\u22612 mod341\u22601. Therefore, contradiction. Wait, but earlier we saw that ord_341(2) is LCM of ord_11(2) and ord_31(2). ord_11(2)=10, ord_31(2)=5, so LCM(10,5)=10. So ord_341(2)=10. But 10 divides 341? No. 341/10=34.1. So ord_n(2) does not divide n, hence 2^n \u22611 modn is not satisfied. Therefore, 341 is out.\n\nSo, in order for ord_n(2) to divide n, which is required because 2^n \u22611 modn implies ord_n(2) divides n, we must have that ord_n(2) divides n and ord_n(2) divides \u03c6(n). Therefore, ord_n(2) divides gcd(n, \u03c6(n)). Therefore, if we can find n such that ord_n(2) divides gcd(n, \u03c6(n)), then it's possible. However, this seems complex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe such n must be 1. Let's test n=1. Yes, works. Let me check if there are others.\n\nAnother approach: suppose n>1 is a solution. Then n is odd, composite. Let p be the smallest prime divisor of n. Then 2^n \u22611 modp. But since p is prime, ord_p(2) divides n and also ord_p(2) divides p-1. Therefore, ord_p(2) divides gcd(n, p-1). But since p is the smallest prime divisor of n, all other prime divisors of n are \u2265p. Therefore, gcd(n, p-1) must divide p-1, which is less than p. But since p is the smallest prime divisor, gcd(n, p-1)=1 or some divisor of p-1. However, ord_p(2) divides gcd(n, p-1). Therefore, ord_p(2) divides p-1 and divides n. So ord_p(2) divides gcd(n, p-1). If gcd(n, p-1)=1, then ord_p(2)=1, which would imply 2\u22611 modp, so p divides 1, which is impossible. Th