spectively. Therefore, the least common multiple of q\u00b2 and q\u00b3 is q\u00b3. So if we take b = q\u00b3, then a = (4p\u00b2 - q\u00b2)*q and c = 4p(2p\u00b2 - q\u00b2). However, we must ensure that 4p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 and 2p\u00b2 - q\u00b2 are positive. \n\nGiven that t = p/q > \u221a2/2, so p/q > \u221a2/2 => p > q*\u221a2/2. Since p and q are integers, this gives a lower bound on p. Also, t < 1, so p < q.\n\nMoreover, from angle conditions, we have angle C = 180\u00b0 -4\u03b8 > 0 => \u03b8 < 45\u00b0, so t = cos\u03b8 > cos45\u00b0 = \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.707, which is already considered.\n\nAdditionally, we need:\n\n1. 4t\u00b2 -1 > 0 => t > 1/2, which is already satisfied since t > \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.707.\n\n2. 2t\u00b2 -1 > 0 => t > \u221a(1/2) \u2248 0.707, which is again our lower bound.\n\nSo, t must satisfy t > \u221a2/2, t < 1, and t = p/q (rational), with p and q positive integers, p > q\u221a2/2, p < q.\n\nBut since p and q are integers, let's try small values of q and find possible p such that p/q > \u221a2/2 and p/q <1.\n\nFor example, let's start with q=2:\n\nq=2: p must satisfy p > 2*(\u221a2/2)=\u221a2\u22481.414, so p=2. But p=2, q=2 gives t=1, which is not less than 1. So invalid.\n\nq=3:\n\np must satisfy p > 3*(\u221a2/2)\u22482.121, so p=3. Then t=3/3=1, again invalid.\n\nq=4:\n\np > 4*(\u221a2/2)=2\u221a2\u22482.828. So p=3. Then t=3/4=0.75. Let's check if 3/4 > \u221a2/2 \u22480.707. 0.75 > 0.707, yes. So t=3/4. Then compute 4t\u00b2 -1 = 4*(9/16) -1 = 9/4 -1 = 5/4. Then 4t(2t\u00b2 -1) = 4*(3/4)*(2*(9/16)-1) = 3*(18/16 -1) = 3*(2/16) = 3*(1/8) = 3/8. \n\nSo, a = b*(5/4), c = b*(3/8). For a and c to be integers, b must be a multiple of 8. Let's take the minimal b=8. Then a=8*(5/4)=10, c=8*(3/8)=3. Therefore, sides would be a=10, b=8, c=3. Wait, but let's check if these sides satisfy the triangle inequality.\n\nCheck:\n\na + b > c: 10 +8=18 >3, yes.\n\na + c > b: 10 +3=13 >8, yes.\n\nb + c > a: 8 +3=11 >10, yes. Okay, so triangle with sides 10,8,3. But perimeter is 10+8+3=21.\n\nBut wait, is that the minimal perimeter? Maybe, but let's check if this triangle actually satisfies the angle conditions. Because we started with t=3/4, but we need to verify that angle A is three times angle B. Let's see.\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines to compute angles. Let's compute angles B and A.\n\nFirst, side a=10 is opposite angle A, side b=8 is opposite angle B, side c=3 is opposite angle C.\n\nCompute angle B using Law of Cosines:\n\ncos B = (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac) = (10\u00b2 +3\u00b2 -8\u00b2)/(2*10*3) = (100 +9 -64)/60 = (45)/60 = 3/4.\n\nTherefore, angle B = arccos(3/4) \u224841.41 degrees. Then angle A = 3\u03b8 \u2248124.23 degrees. Then angle C = 180 - 3\u03b8 - \u03b8 = 180 -4\u03b8 \u2248180 -165.64=14.36 degrees. Let's check Law of Sines:\n\na/sin A \u224810/sin(124.23\u00b0)=10/0.8253\u224812.12\n\nb/sin B \u22488/sin(41.41\u00b0)=8/0.664\u224812.05\n\nc/sin C \u22483/sin(14.36\u00b0)=3/0.248\u224812.10\n\nThese are approximately equal, considering rounding errors. So this seems valid.\n\nSo perimeter 21. But is this the minimal? Let's check with smaller q.\n\nWait, q=4 gives us b=8. Maybe there's a smaller q. Let's check q=5.\n\nq=5: p must satisfy p>5*(\u221a2/2)\u22483.535. So p=4. Then t=4/5=0.8.\n\nCompute 4t\u00b2 -1=4*(16/25)-1=64/25 -1=39/25=1.56\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(4/5)*(2*(16/25)-1)= (16/5)*(32/25 -1)= (16/5)*(7/25)=112/125\u22480.896\n\nTherefore, a = b*(39/25), c = b*(112/125). To make a and c integers, b must be a multiple of 125. Let b=125. Then a=125*(39/25)=195, c=125*(112/125)=112. Perimeter=195+125+112=432. That's way bigger than 21. So not better.\n\nq=5, p=4 gives a larger perimeter. What about q=7?\n\nWait, let's try q=5. Wait maybe q=7:\n\nWait, perhaps trying q=5, p=4. Wait we did that. Let's try q=6:\n\nq=6. p must be >6*(\u221a2/2)=3\u221a2\u22484.242. So p=5. t=5/6\u22480.833.\n\nCompute 4t\u00b2 -1=4*(25/36)-1=100/36 -1=64/36=16/9\u22481.777\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(5/6)*(2*(25/36)-1)= (20/6)*(50/36 -1)= (10/3)*(14/36)= (10/3)*(7/18)=70/54\u22481.296\n\nThus, a = b*(16/9), c = b*(70/54)=b*(35/27). To make a and c integers, b must be multiple of 27 and 9. LCM(27,9)=27. So take b=27. Then a=27*(16/9)=48, c=27*(35/27)=35. Perimeter=48+27+35=110. Still larger than 21.\n\nHmm, so q=4 gives perimeter 21. Let me check q=3. Wait, q=3: p must be >3*(\u221a2/2)\u22482.121, so p=3. Then t=3/3=1, which is invalid. So no solution for q=3. q=2 similarly invalid. So q=4 is the smallest possible q where t is rational and in the valid range. So with q=4, we get perimeter 21. \n\nBut wait, is there a way to get a triangle with smaller perimeter? Maybe with non-rational t but such that the ratios a/b and c/b are rational. Because we assumed t is rational, but maybe t isn't rational, but the ratios 4t\u00b2 -1 and 4t(2t\u00b2 -1) are rational. For example, if t is irrational but 4t\u00b2 -1 and 4t(2t\u00b2 -1) are rational. Let's explore that possibility.\n\nLet\u2019s denote u = t. Then we have:\n\na/b = 4u\u00b2 -1\n\nc/b = 4u(2u\u00b2 -1)\n\nLet\u2019s denote these as rational numbers m and n, respectively. So:\n\n4u\u00b2 -1 = m\n\n4u(2u\u00b2 -1) = n\n\nWhere m and n are positive rational numbers (since a, b, c are positive integers). Then, we can write:\n\nFrom the first equation: u\u00b2 = (m +1)/4\n\nSubstitute into the second equation:\n\n4u(2*( (m +1)/4 ) -1 ) = n\n\nSimplify inside the parentheses:\n\n2*( (m +1)/4 ) -1 = (m +1)/2 -1 = (m +1 -2)/2 = (m -1)/2\n\nTherefore:\n\n4u*( (m -1)/2 ) = n => 2u(m -1) = n\n\nBut from the first equation, u\u00b2 = (m +1)/4 => u = sqrt( (m +1)/4 ). So u is sqrt((m+1)/4). Therefore, substituting into the second equation:\n\n2*sqrt( (m +1)/4 )*(m -1) = n => sqrt( (m +1)/4 )*(m -1) = n/2\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n( (m +1)/4 )*(m -1)^2 = n\u00b2/4\n\nMultiply both sides by 4:\n\n(m +1)(m -1)^2 = n\u00b2\n\nSo, (m\u00b2 -1)(m -1) = n\u00b2 => (m -1)(m +1)(m -1) = n\u00b2 => (m -1)^2(m +1) = n\u00b2\n\nThus, (m -1)^2(m +1) must be a perfect square. Let\u2019s denote k\u00b2 = (m -1)^2(m +1). Then k = (m -1)sqrt(m +1). Therefore, sqrt(m +1) must be rational. Let\u2019s set sqrt(m +1) = p/q where p and q are coprime integers. Then m +1 = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 => m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1. Substituting back into k:\n\nk = (m -1)sqrt(m +1) = ( (p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1 ) -1 )*(p/q) = (p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -2)*(p/q) = (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)/q\u00b2 * p/q = p(p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)/q\u00b3\n\nBut k must be rational, so p(p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)/q\u00b3 must be rational. Since p and q are integers, this is automatically rational. However, we need (m -1)^2(m +1) to be a perfect square. Since m -1 = (p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1 -1) = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -2, so:\n\n(m -1)^2(m +1) = (p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -2)^2*(p\u00b2/q\u00b2)\n\n= [ (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)/q\u00b2 ]\u00b2 * p\u00b2/q\u00b2\n\n= (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)^2 * p\u00b2 / q^6\n\nThis must be equal to (n)^2 = (some rational number)^2. Therefore, the numerator must be a perfect square. Let\u2019s write:\n\n(numerator) = (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)^2 * p\u00b2\n\nDenominator = q^6\n\nThus, (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)^2 * p\u00b2 must be a perfect square. Since both (p\u00b2 - 2q\u00b2)^2 and p\u00b2 are squares, their product is a square. Therefore, the entire expression is a square, so n is rational. Therefore, for any integers p and q, this holds. Therefore, to make m and n rational, we need m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1 and n as defined above.\n\nBut m and n must also be positive. Since m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1, we need p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1 >0 => p\u00b2 > q\u00b2 => p>q. Also, from the earlier condition t = sqrt( (m +1)/4 ) = sqrt( p\u00b2/q\u00b2 /4 ) = p/(2q). Wait, t = sqrt( (m +1)/4 ) = sqrt( (p\u00b2/q\u00b2)/4 ) = p/(2q). Therefore, t = p/(2q). But earlier, we had t = cos\u03b8 \u2208 (\u221a2/2,1). So p/(2q) > \u221a2/2 => p/q > \u221a2. Since p > q (from m >0), and p/q > \u221a2. So p and q are positive integers with p > q\u221a2.\n\nWait, so t = p/(2q), and t must be less than 1, so p/(2q) <1 => p < 2q. Therefore, combining these inequalities:\n\nq\u221a2 < p < 2q.\n\nTherefore, for a given q, p must be an integer in (q\u221a2, 2q). Let\u2019s find integers q and p such that q\u221a2 < p <2q.\n\nFor q=2:\n\nq\u221a2\u22482.828 < p <4. So p=3.\n\nq=3:\n\n3\u221a2\u22484.242 <p <6. So p=5.\n\nq=4:\n\n4\u221a2\u22485.656 <p <8. So p=6,7.\n\nq=5:\n\n5\u221a2\u22487.071 <p <10. So p=8,9.\n\nq=1:\n\n1\u221a2\u22481.414 <p <2. p=2, but p must be less than 2, so no solution.\n\nSo let's start with q=2, p=3:\n\nt = p/(2q) =3/(4)=0.75. Which is the case we considered earlier. Then m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1=9/4 -1=5/4. Then a = b*(5/4), c= b*(3/8). So perimeter=10+8+3=21 as before.\n\nNext, q=3, p=5:\n\nt=5/(6)\u22480.833. Then m=25/9 -1=16/9. So a =b*(16/9), c= b*(4t(2t\u00b2 -1)). Compute 2t\u00b2 -1=2*(25/36)-1=50/36 -1=14/36=7/18. Then 4t*(7/18)=4*(5/6)*(7/18)= (20/6)*(7/18)= (10/3)*(7/18)=70/54=35/27. So c =b*(35/27). Therefore, if we take b=27, a=27*(16/9)=48, c=35. Perimeter=48+27+35=110, which is the same as when we set t=5/6 earlier. So perimeter 110.\n\nq=4, p=6:\n\nt=6/(8)=0.75. Wait, same as q=2, p=3. Since 6/8=3/4. So same case. Redundant.\n\nq=4, p=7:\n\nt=7/8\u22480.875. Then m=49/16 -1=33/16. Then a =b*(33/16). Compute c:\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(7/8)*(2*(49/64)-1)= (28/8)*(98/64 -1)= (3.5)*(98/64 -64/64)=3.5*(34/64)=3.5*(17/32)= (7/2)*(17/32)=119/64\u22481.859. So c =b*(119/64). To make a and c integers, b must be multiple of 64 and 16. LCM(64,16)=64. So b=64. Then a=64*(33/16)=132, c=64*(119/64)=119. Perimeter=132+64+119=315. Larger than 21.\n\nq=5, p=8:\n\nt=8/10=0.8. Then m=64/25 -1=39/25. a =b*(39/25). Compute c:\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(8/10)*(2*(64/100)-1)= (32/10)*(128/100 -1)=3.2*(28/100)=3.2*0.28=0.896=112/125. So c =b*(112/125). Take b=125, a=195, c=112. Perimeter=195+125+112=432.\n\nq=5, p=9:\n\nt=9/10=0.9. Then m=81/25 -1=56/25. a =b*(56/25). Compute c:\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(9/10)*(2*(81/100)-1)= (36/10)*(162/100 -1)=3.6*(62/100)=3.6*0.62=2.232=558/250=279/125. So c =b*(279/125). Then take b=125, a=125*(56/25)=280, c=279. Perimeter=280+125+279=684. Still larger.\n\nThus, so far, the minimal perimeter found is 21 with sides 10,8,3. But let me check if there's a smaller q and p that I might have missed. For q=2, p=3 gives perimeter 21. Let me check q=7, p=10:\n\nWait, q=7: p must be >7\u221a2\u22489.899, p<14. So p=10,11,12,13. Let's check p=10:\n\nt=10/(14)=5/7\u22480.714. Check if 5/7>\u221a2/2\u22480.707. Yes, 5/7\u22480.714>0.707. Then m=25/49 -1= (25 -49)/49= -24/49. Wait, negative. Which is invalid because m must be positive since a = b*(m). So invalid. So p must be such that m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1 >0 => p\u00b2 > q\u00b2 => p>q. But for q=7, p must be between ~9.899 and14. p=10,11,12,13.\n\nBut m = p\u00b2/q\u00b2 -1. For p=10, q=7: m=100/49 -1\u22482.04 -1=1.04>0. So m=51/49. Then a =b*(51/49). Compute c:\n\n4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=4*(10/14)*(2*(100/196)-1)= (40/14)*(200/196 -1)= (20/7)*(4/196)= (20/7)*(1/49)=20/343\u22480.058. So c =b*(20/343). Therefore, to make a and c integers, b must be multiple of 343 and 49. LCM(343,49)=343. Take b=343. Then a=343*(51/49)=357, c=343*(20/343)=20. Perimeter=357+343+20=720. Still much larger than 21.\n\nHmm. So seems like q=2,p=3 gives the smallest perimeter of 21. But let's check if there's a non-rational t which could give smaller perimeter. For example, maybe t is irrational but the ratios 4t\u00b2 -1 and 4t(2t\u00b2 -1) are rational. But how?\n\nIf t is such that 4t\u00b2 -1 = r/s and 4t(2t\u00b2 -1)=u/v where r,s,u,v are integers, then t would satisfy some polynomial equations. Let\u2019s suppose 4t\u00b2 -1 = m and 4t(2t\u00b2 -1) = n, where m and n are rational numbers. Then, from the first equation, t\u00b2=(m +1)/4. Substitute into the second equation:\n\n4t(2*( (m +1)/4 ) -1 )=n\n\n=> 4t*( (m +1)/2 -1 ) =n\n\n=> 4t*( (m -1)/2 ) =n\n\n=> 2t(m -1)=n\n\nBut from t\u00b2=(m +1)/4, t= sqrt( (m +1)/4 ). So:\n\n2*sqrt( (m +1)/4 )*(m -1)=n\n\n=> sqrt( (m +1)/4 )*(m -1)=n/2\n\n=> sqrt(m +1)*(m -1)=n\n\nThus, sqrt(m +1)*(m -1) must be rational. Let\u2019s set m +1 = k\u00b2, where k is rational. Then sqrt(m +1)=k, so m =k\u00b2 -1. Then:\n\nk*(k\u00b2 -1 -1)=k*(k\u00b2 -2)=n\n\nThus, n= k\u00b3 -2k. Therefore, m and n can be parameterized by k. Since m and n must be rational, k must be rational. Therefore, we can set k = p/q where p and q are integers, leading to m = (p/q)\u00b2 -1 and n=(p/q)\u00b3 -2*(p/q). \n\nBut this seems similar to the previous approach. Therefore, the minimal solution would correspond to the minimal k such that t = sqrt( (m +1)/4 ) = sqrt( k\u00b2 /4 )= k/2. Thus, t= k/2. But t must be in (\u221a2/2,1), so k must be in (\u221a2,2). Since k is rational, k= p/q where p and q are integers with p/q \u2208 (\u221a2,2). The minimal such fractions would correspond to the minimal q where p/q is in that interval. \n\nFor example, the minimal fractions greater than \u221a2 (~1.414) are 3/2=1.5, 4/3\u22481.333 (which is less than \u221a2), 5/3\u22481.666, etc. So k=3/2 is the smallest rational number greater than \u221a2. Then, k=3/2. Let's compute m and n:\n\nm =k\u00b2 -1=9/4 -1=5/4\n\nn= k\u00b3 -2k= (27/8) -3=27/8 -24/8=3/8\n\nThus, a =b*m =b*(5/4), c =b*n =b*(3/8). Therefore, b must be a multiple of 8 to have a and c integers. Taking b=8 gives a=10, c=3. Which is the same triangle as before with perimeter 21. \n\nIf we take the next k=5/3\u22481.666:\n\nm=(25/9)-1=16/9\n\nn=(125/27)-10/3=125/27 -90/27=35/27\n\nThen a =b*(16/9), c =b*(35/27). LCM of denominators 9 and27 is27. So b=27 gives a=48, c=35. Perimeter=48+27+35=110, which is the same as before.\n\nNext k=4/3\u22481.333, but that's less than \u221a2, so invalid. Next k=5/3\u22481.666. Then k=7/4=1.75:\n\nm=(49/16)-1=33/16\n\nn=(343/64)-14/4=343/64 -224/64=119/64\n\nSo a=33/16*b, c=119/64*b. LCM(16,64)=64. b=64 gives a=132, c=119. Perimeter=132+64+119=315. Same as before.\n\nThus, the minimal solution is indeed when k=3/2, leading to perimeter 21. \n\nBut wait, before concluding, let's check if there are any other triangles with smaller perimeters that maybe don't fit into this parameterization. For example, perhaps the triangle with sides 10,8,3 is indeed the minimal one. Let's check another approach.\n\nSuppose we use the Law of Sines ratios:\n\na / sin3\u03b8 = b / sin\u03b8 = c / sin4\u03b8.\n\nLet\u2019s denote this common ratio as k. So:\n\na =k sin3\u03b8,\n\nb =k sin\u03b8,\n\nc =k sin4\u03b8.\n\nSince sides a, b, c must be integers, k must be chosen such that k sin\u03b8, k sin3\u03b8, k sin4\u03b8 are all integers. Let\u2019s denote k as 1/m, where m is the greatest common divisor to make the sides integers. Wait, but this might complicate. Alternatively, think of k as a scaling factor. The sides a,b,c are proportional to sin3\u03b8, sin\u03b8, sin4\u03b8. To make them integers, we can find a common multiple such that sin3\u03b8, sin\u03b8, sin4\u03b8 are rational multiples. \n\nBut this seems similar to previous approaches. Alternatively, use specific angle values. For example, take \u03b8 such that cos\u03b8 is rational. For instance, we had \u03b8 where cos\u03b8=3/4, leading to the sides 10,8,3. Alternatively, \u03b8 with cos\u03b8=5/6, but that led to larger sides. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the Law of Cosines for angle relations. Let's try another approach. Let's denote angle B=\u03b8, so angle A=3\u03b8, angle C=180\u00b0-4\u03b8. Using the Law of Cosines on angles A and B.\n\nFor angle A=3\u03b8:\n\na\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc cos3\u03b8\n\nFor angle B=\u03b8:\n\nb\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2ac cos\u03b8\n\nHmm, but this seems complicated. Alternatively, express cos3\u03b8 in terms of cos\u03b8. Recall that cos3\u03b8=4cos\u00b3\u03b8 -3cos\u03b8. Let\u2019s denote t=cos\u03b8. Then cos3\u03b8=4t\u00b3 -3t.\n\nThus, from Law of Cosines:\n\na\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t)\n\nBut we also have from angle B=\u03b8:\n\nb\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2ac*t\n\nSo we have two equations:\n\n1. a\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t)\n\n2. b\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2ac*t\n\nThis system seems complex. Let\u2019s see if we can substitute variables. From equation 2:\n\nb\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2ac*t => 2ac*t = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2 => t = (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac)\n\nSimilarly, from equation 1:\n\na\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t) => a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 = -2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t) => 2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t) = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 -a\u00b2\n\nBut substituting t from equation 2:\n\nt = (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac)\n\nTherefore, 4t\u00b3 -3t = 4[(a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac)]\u00b3 -3[(a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac)]\n\nThis expression would need to be substituted into the equation:\n\n2bc*(4t\u00b3 -3t) = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 -a\u00b2\n\nThis seems very messy. Perhaps not the best approach. Let me think of a specific example. We have the triangle with sides 10,8,3. Let's verify angles using Law of Cosines.\n\nCompute angle B (opposite side b=8):\n\ncos B = (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - b\u00b2)/(2ac) = (100 +9 -64)/(2*10*3)=45/60=3/4. So angle B= arccos(3/4)\u224841.41 degrees.\n\nAngle A=3\u03b8\u2248124.23 degrees. Compute cos A=cos(3\u03b8)=4cos\u00b3\u03b8 -3cos\u03b8=4*(3/4)^3 -3*(3/4)=4*(27/64) -9/4=108/64 -144/64= -36/64= -9/16.\n\nCheck using Law of Cosines for angle A:\n\ncos A = (b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 -a\u00b2)/(2bc)= (64 +9 -100)/(2*8*3)= (-27)/48= -9/16. Which matches. Therefore, this triangle is valid.\n\nNow, to check if there is a smaller perimeter triangle with integer sides satisfying angle A=3\u03b8. Let's see if there exists a triangle with perimeter less than 21. The minimal perimeter for a triangle is 3 (1,1,1), but obviously, angles in an equilateral triangle are all 60\u00b0, so doesn't satisfy the condition. \n\nThe next possible perimeters are higher. Let's check smaller sides. For example, perimeter 12: possible triangles like 3,4,5. Let's see 