ord length is 2, that would contribute more to the total sum.\n\nBut wait, in a regular pentagon, the diagonals are longer than the sides. So maybe a regular pentagon is already a good candidate. But maybe there's a way to cluster some points closer together to allow others to be further apart, thereby creating longer chords and diagonals.\n\nWait, but all points must lie on the circle. So if we cluster some points closer, that would allow other points to be spaced further apart. For example, if three points are close together, the remaining two points can be placed opposite to that cluster, creating longer chords. But since the circle is symmetric, maybe such an arrangement might lead to some longer chords but others being shorter. The total sum might depend on the balance between the longer and shorter chords.\n\nAlternatively, maybe distributing the points as evenly as possible (i.e., regular pentagon) maximizes the sum because each chord is as long as possible given the spacing. If you cluster points, some chords become longer, but others become shorter. Perhaps the regular arrangement gives the optimal balance.\n\nBut I need to verify this. Let's consider the problem mathematically.\n\nLet me denote the five points on the circle by their angles \u03b8\u2081, \u03b8\u2082, \u03b8\u2083, \u03b8\u2084, \u03b8\u2085 in radians, measured from some fixed axis. The chord length between two points with angles \u03b8_i and \u03b8_j is 2*sin(|\u03b8_i - \u03b8_j| / 2). The total sum we want to maximize is the sum over all edges (consecutive points) and all diagonals (non-consecutive points). In total, for five points, each pair of points is either a side or a diagonal. Wait, actually, the problem says the sum of the perimeter and the five diagonals. Wait, perimeter is the sum of the five sides, and then adding the five diagonals. Wait, but in a pentagon, there are five diagonals. So the total sum is perimeter (5 sides) + 5 diagonals. So in total, 10 line segments? Wait, 