, P, Q, R, S lie on a circle.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to proving that AR * AS = AP^2.\n\nGiven the conditions \u2220BPS = \u2220PRS and \u2220CQR = \u2220QSR, we need to derive that AR * AS = AP^2.\n\nTo do this, we can use the Law of Sines in triangles involving these angles.\n\nLet me consider triangle BPS and triangle PRS.\n\nGiven \u2220BPS = \u2220PRS = \u03b1.\n\nIn triangle BPS:\n\nBP / sin(\u2220BSP) = PS / sin(\u2220B) = BS / sin(\u03b1)\n\nIn triangle PRS:\n\nPR / sin(\u2220PSR) = RS / sin(\u03b1) = PS / sin(\u2220RPS)\n\nSimilarly, in triangles CQR and QSR:\n\nGiven \u2220CQR = \u2220QSR = \u03b2.\n\nIn triangle CQR:\n\nCQ / sin(\u2220QSR) = QR / sin(\u2220C) = CR / sin(\u03b2)\n\nIn triangle QSR:\n\nQS / sin(\u2220QRS) = SR / sin(\u03b2) = QR / sin(\u2220SQR)\n\nBut this seems too vague. Let me try to relate the sides.\n\nFrom triangle BPS:\n\nBP / sin(\u2220BSP) = PS / sin(\u2220B)\n\nFrom triangle PRS:\n\nPR / sin(\u2220PSR) = RS / sin(\u03b1)\n\nBut \u2220BSP and \u2220PSR are related if points are concyclic, but we don't know that yet.\n\nAlternatively, from the first condition:\n\nBP / sin(\u2220BSP) = PS / sin(\u2220B)\n\nSimilarly, from the second condition in triangle PRS:\n\nPR / sin(\u2220PSR) = RS / sin(\u03b1)\n\nBut \u03b1 is \u2220BPS = \u2220PRS.\n\nAssuming that \u2220BSP = \u2220PSR, then we can relate BP/PR = PS/RS.\n\nBut unless we can prove \u2220BSP = \u2220PSR, which requires cyclic quadrilateral, which is what we need to prove.\n\nThis is circular.\n\nAlternatively, use Ceva's theorem in some way.\n\nAlternatively, since AP = AQ, maybe use the Ratio Lemma (also known as the Law of Sines in a triangle with a cevian).\n\nBut I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, consider the following:\n\nLet\u2019s denote that AP = AQ = x.\n\nThen, BP = AB - x, CQ = AC - x.\n\nPoints R and S are on BC such that \u2220BPS = \u2220PRS and \u2220CQR = \u2220QSR.\n\nLet\u2019s apply the Law of Sines in triangles BPS and PRS.\n\nIn triangle BPS:\n\nB