ere \\( \\alpha' \\in \\{0, 1, \\ldots, \\alpha\\} \\), \\( \\beta_i' \\in \\{0, 1, \\ldots, \\beta_i\\} \\) for \\( 1 \\leq i \\leq k \\), and \\( \\gamma_j' \\in \\{0, 1, \\ldots, \\gamma_j\\} \\) for \\( 1 \\leq j \\leq l \\).\n\n5. **Consider \\( m \\) being odd:**\n\n   If \\( m \\) is odd, \\( f_0(m) = f_2(m) = 0 \\). Using the conclusions above,\n   \\[\n   2017 = f_1(m) - f_3(m) = 2f_1(m) - PQ = P \\left(2 \\left\\lceil \\frac{Q}{2} \\right\\rceil - Q \\right).\n   \\]\n   \n   Since \\( 2 \\left\\lceil \\frac{Q}{2} \\right\\rceil - Q = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   P = 2017.\n   \\]\n   \n   Thus, because 2017 is prime,\n   \\[\n   k = 1, \\quad \\beta_1 = 2016.\n   \\]\n   \n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   m \\geq p_1^{2016} \\prod_{j=1}^l q_j^{\\gamma_j} = 5^{2016}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **If \\( m \\) is even:**\n\n   Using conclusions (1), (2), and (3):\n   \\[\n   2017 = f_0(m) + f_1(m) - f_2(m) - f_3(m) = (\\alpha-1)PQ + P \\left\\lceil \\frac{Q}{2} \\right\\rceil - PQ - PQ + 2P \\left\\lceil \\frac{Q}{2} \\right\\rceil.\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   2017 = P \\left( (\\alpha - 3)Q + 2 \\left\\lceil \\frac{Q}{2} \\right\\rceil \\right).\n   \\]\n   \n   Since 2017 is prime, \\( (\\alpha - 3)Q \\) must be an odd integer, hence \\( Q \\) is odd:\n   \\[\n   (\\alpha - 2)Q = 2016 = 2^5 \\times 3^2 \\times 7.\n   \\]\n   \n   This implies \\( 2^5 \\mid (\\alpha - 2) \\), and checking the smallest solutions:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha - 2 = 32 \\quad \\Rightarrow  Q = 63.\n   \\]\n   \n   \\( l = 3 \\) and possible permutations, giving:\n   \\[\n   2^{34} \\times 3^6 \\times 7^2 \\times 11^2.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Combining the results, the smallest possible \\( m \\) is:\n   \\[\n   m = 2^{34} \\times 3^6 \\times 7^2 \\times 11^2.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive integer \\( m \\) satisfying the condition is:\n\\[ \\boxed{m = 2^{34} \\times 3^6 \\times 7^2 \\times 11^2}. \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_co