ger, but divided by larger denominators. It's not clear if the overall expression increases or decreases. But since in the minimal case S =2 gives 1/3, and for S >2, even if variables are spread out, the expression might be larger.\n\nAlternatively, consider using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality on the expression \u03a3 [w\u00b3/(S - w)]:\n\n\u03a3 [w\u00b3/(S - w)] = \u03a3 [w^4 / (w(S - w))] \u2265 (\u03a3 w\u00b2)^2 / \u03a3 [w(S - w)]\n\nFrom Cauchy-Schwarz, this inequality holds. So we have:\n\n\u03a3 [w\u00b3/(S - w)] \u2265 (\u03a3 w\u00b2)^2 / [S \u03a3 w - \u03a3 w\u00b2] = (\u03a3 w\u00b2)^2 / [S\u00b2 - \u03a3 w\u00b2]\n\nLet\u2019s denote \u03a3 w\u00b2 = Q. Then, the inequality becomes:\n\n\u03a3 [w\u00b3/(S - w)] \u2265 Q\u00b2 / (S\u00b2 - Q)\n\nWe need to show that Q\u00b2 / (S\u00b2 - Q) \u2265 1/3.\n\nSo:\n\nQ\u00b2 / (S\u00b2 - Q) \u2265 1/3 \u21d2 3Q\u00b2 \u2265 S\u00b2 - Q \u21d2 3Q\u00b2 + Q - S\u00b2 \u2265 0\n\nWe need to relate Q and S. From the given condition, we have (a + c)(b + d) =1, where a =w, c =y, b =x, d =z. And S = a + b + c + d.\n\nBut from Cauchy-Schwarz, we know that (a\u00b2 + c\u00b2)(1 +1) \u2265 (a + c)^2 \u21d2 Q1 = a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 \u2265 p\u00b2 /2, where p =a +c. Similarly, b\u00b2 + d\u00b2 \u2265 q\u00b2 /2, where q = b + d. Therefore, Q = Q1 + Q2 \u2265 p\u00b2/2 + q\u00b2/2. But since pq =1, we have Q \u2265 (p\u00b2 + q\u00b2)/2.\n\nBut p + q = S, and pq =1. Let\u2019s express p\u00b2 + q\u00b2 = (p + q)^2 - 2pq = S\u00b2 - 2. So Q \u2265 (S\u00b2 - 2)/2.\n\nTherefore, Q \u2265 (S\u00b2 -2)/2.\n\nSubstitute into the inequality 3Q\u00b2 + Q - S\u00b2 \u2265 0:\n\nIf Q \u2265 (S\u00b2 -2)/2, then 3Q\u00b2 + Q - S\u00b2 \u2265 3[(S\u00b2 -2)/2]^2 + (S\u00b2 -2)/2 - S\u00b2.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst term: 3*((S\u00b2 -2)^2)/4 = (3(S^4 -4S\u00b2 +4))/4\n\nSecond term: (S\u00b2 -2)/2\n\nThird term: -S\u00b2\n\nSo overall:\n\n(3(S^4 -4S\u00b2 +4))/4 + (S\u00b2 -2)/2 - S\u00b2\n\nConvert to quarters:\n\n[3S^4 -12S\