e tangent plane at a point on a cylinder (which is a developable surface) touches the cylinder along a generator line. But in this case, the plane is not a tangent along a generator but at a single point each on the top and bottom circles.\n\nWait, actually, for a cylinder, a tangent plane at a point (x, y, z) must satisfy the condition that the gradient of the cylinder's equation is orthogonal to the plane. The cylinder's equation is \\( x^2 + y^2 = 1 \\). The gradient at any point (x, y, z) is (2x, 2y, 0). So the tangent plane at a point (a, b, c) on the cylinder is \\( 2a(x - a) + 2b(y - b) = 0 \\), simplifying to \\( a x + b y = a^2 + b^2 = 1 \\), since \\( a^2 + b^2 = 1 \\). So the tangent plane at any point (a, b, c) on the cylinder is \\( a x + b y = 1 \\).\n\nBut our plane must be tangent to both the top circle \\( z = h \\) and the bottom circle \\( z = 0 \\). Wait, but the tangent plane as derived is vertical\u2014doesn't involve z-coordinate. That seems to be a problem. Wait, actually, the cylinder is infinite in the z-direction, but here we have finite height. So the plane is tangent to the top and bottom circles, which are the circles at z = 0 and z = h with radius 1. However, the standard tangent plane to the cylinder (the lateral surface) is vertical, but here the plane is tangent to the circular edges at the top and bottom. So maybe we need a different approach.\n\nWait, the top and bottom circles are in the planes z = h and z = 0, each with radius 1. The plane we are looking for is tangent to both of these circles. So, for instance, a plane tangent to the top circle at a point (a, b, h) and tangent to the bottom circle at a point (c, d, 0), and passing through the center (0,0,h/2).\n\nLet me recall that a tangent plane to a circle in 3D. The top circle is in the plane z = h, centered at (0,0,h), radius 1. A tangent plane to this circle at point (a, b, h) must satisfy that the vector from the center to the point (a, b, h) is perpendicular to the plan