u1d62\u00b2 = |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2, then \u03a3 k\u1d62\u00b2 = n ( r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 ) = n r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n.\n\nTherefore, we have \u03a3 k\u1d62\u00b2 = n r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n.\n\nBut we also have \u03a3 k\u1d62 = 0.\n\nThese are the same conditions as having a set of numbers k\u2081, k\u2082, ..., k\u2099 with sum zero and sum of squares equal to n r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n.\n\nThe minimum possible value of \u03a3 k\u1d62\u00b2 given \u03a3 k\u1d62 = 0 is achieved when all k\u1d62 are equal, but since \u03a3 k\u1d62 = 0, this would require all k\u1d62 = 0. Hence, the minimal sum of squares is zero. Therefore,\n\nn r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n \u2265 0 \u21d2 |S|\u00b2 \u2264 n\u00b2 r\u00b2.\n\nBut we also have from earlier:\n\nr\u00b2 = |a\u1d62|\u00b2 = (c / |S| )\u00b2 + |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2 = ( |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 ) + |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2.\n\nBut |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - ( |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 ) \u2265 0 \u21d2 |S|\u00b2 \u2264 n\u00b2 r\u00b2.\n\nSo equality holds when |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2 = 0, i.e., when all a\u1d62 are colinear with S. However, as discussed earlier, this is impossible for distinct vectors with n > 2.\n\nTherefore, |S|\u00b2 < n\u00b2 r\u00b2, and hence n r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n > 0.\n\nBut returning to \u03a3 k\u1d62\u00b2 = n r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n, and \u03a3 k\u1d62 = 0. The only way this can happen is if the k\u1d62 are arranged such that their squares add up to this value while summing to zero. However, in one dimension (since the perpendicular components are colinear), this requires that for every positive k\u1d62, there's a corresponding negative k\u2c7c. But given that all |a\u1d62\u22a5| are equal (since |a\u1d62\u22a5|\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 ), this means that all k\u1d62 have the same magnitude. Hence, each k\u1d62 is either s or -s, where s = sqrt(r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 ).\n\nTherefore, the perpendicular components are either s \u015d\u22a5 or -s \u015d\u22a5, and their sum is zero. Hence, there must be an equal number of positive and negative terms. Therefore, n must be even, and half of the k\u1d62 are s, half are -s. But the problem allows n > 2, which includes both even and odd n.\n\nHowever, if n is odd, this is impossible. For example, if n=3, you can't split 3 into two equal groups. Therefore, for odd n, the only way \u03a3 k\u1d62 = 0 with all |k\u1d62| equal is if all k\u1d62 = 0, which implies s = 0, so r\u00b2 - |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 = 0 \u21d2 |S|\u00b2 = n\u00b2 r\u00b2 \u21d2 |S| = n r. But then, from |S|\u00b2 = n c, we have n\u00b2 r\u00b2 = n c \u21d2 c = n r\u00b2. But c = S \u00b7 a\u1d62 = |S| r cos \u03b8 = n r * r cos \u03b8 = n r\u00b2 cos \u03b8. Therefore, n r\u00b2 cos \u03b8 = n r\u00b2 \u21d2 cos \u03b8 = 1 \u21d2 \u03b8 = 0. Therefore, all a\u1d62 are colinear with S, i.e., a\u1d62 = r \u015d. But this contradicts the distinctness of the vectors. Therefore, for odd n, the only solution is S=0.\n\nFor even n, we could have s \u2260 0 with an equal number of positive and negative perpendicular components. However, in this case, the sum of the parallel components would be S = n (c / |S| ) \u015d = n ( |S|\u00b2 / n\u00b2 |S| ) \u015d = n ( |S| / n\u00b2 ) \u015d = ( |S| / n ) \u015d. But S is also equal to |S| \u015d. Therefore,\n\n|S| \u015d = ( |S| / n ) \u015d \u21d2 |S| = |S| / n \u21d2 |S| (1 - 1/n ) = 0 \u21d2 |S| = 0.\n\nTherefore, even for even n, the only solution is S=0.\n\nTherefore, regardless of whether n is even or odd, the only possible solution is S=0.\n\nThis closes the loophole I found earlier where I thought a colinear configuration might work, but in reality, due to the distinctness requirement and the parity of n, such configurations are impossible unless S=0.\n\nTherefore, combining all these observations, we conclude that S must be zero.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\mathbf{0}}", "deepseek_solution": "Given \\( n > 2 \\) distinct vectors \\(\\boldsymbol{a}_1, \\boldsymbol{a}_2, \\ldots, \\boldsymbol{a}_n\\) on a plane with equal lengths, we need to prove that \\(\\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n = \\mathbf{0}\\) under the condition that all vectors \\(-\\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n, \\boldsymbol{a}_1 - \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\boldsymbol{a}_3 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n, \\ldots, \\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_{n-1} - \\boldsymbol{a}_n\\) have equal lengths.\n\n1. **Notation and Setup**:\n   - Let \\( S = \\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n \\).\n   - Each vector \\( S - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i \\) has the same length \\( L \\).\n\n2. **Magnitude Analysis**:\n   - The magnitude squared of \\( S - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i \\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     |S - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i|^2 = |S|^2 - 4S \\cdot \\boldsymbol{a}_i + 4|\\boldsymbol{a}_i|^2\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( |\\boldsymbol{a}_i| = r \\) (constant), we have:\n     \\[\n     |S|^2 - 4S \\cdot \\boldsymbol{a}_i + 4r^2 = L^2\n     \\]\n   - This implies \\( S \\cdot \\boldsymbol{a}_i = c \\) (constant) for all \\( i \\).\n\n3. **Dot Product and Sum**:\n   - The dot product \\( S \\cdot \\boldsymbol{a}_i = c \\) leads to:\n     \\[\n     S \\cdot S = |S|^2 = n c\n     \\]\n   - Summing \\( S \\cdot \\boldsymbol{a}_i \\) over all \\( i \\) gives:\n     \\[\n     |S|^2 = n c \\implies c = \\frac{|S|^2}{n}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Decomposition into Components**:\n   - Each \\(\\boldsymbol{a}_i\\) is decomposed into parallel and perpendicular components relative to \\( S \\):\n     \\[\n     \\boldsymbol{a}_i = \\left( \\frac{c}{|S|} \\right) \\mathbf{\\hat{s}} + \\boldsymbol{a}_i^\\perp\n     \\]\n   - The sum of perpendicular components must be zero:\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{i=1}^n \\boldsymbol{a}_i^\\perp = \\mathbf{0}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Geometric Interpretation**:\n   - The perpendicular components \\(\\boldsymbol{a}_i^\\perp\\) are vectors of equal magnitude arranged symmetrically around the origin, forming a closed polygon.\n   - For \\( n \\) vectors in a plane with equal magnitude and summing to zero, they must form a regular polygon, implying their sum \\( S \\) is zero.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - Considering the distinctness and equal length of vectors, the only configuration where the sum of vectors \\( S \\) and their perpendicular components cancel out is when \\( S = \\mathbf{0} \\).\n\nThus, we conclude that the sum of all vectors is zero:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathbf{0}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. First, let's consider vectors $2\\boldsymbol{a}_1, 2\\boldsymbol{a}_2, \\ldots, 2\\boldsymbol{a}_n$ originating from a single point $O$ such that $\\overrightarrow{OA}_i = 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i$ for $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n$.\n2. Since these vectors all have the same length, the points $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ lie on a circle $\\omega$ centered at $O$. Let's call the length of these vectors $R$, hence $|2\\boldsymbol{a}_i| = R$.\n\n3. Let $\\boldsymbol{s} = \\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n$.\n4. According to the problem, the vectors $\\boldsymbol{s} - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i$ all have the same length $r$. Thus, for $i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n$, \n   \\[\n   |\\boldsymbol{s} - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i| = r\n   \\]\n\n5. Consider a point $S$ such that $\\overrightarrow{OS} = \\boldsymbol{s}$.\n6. We know from the setup that $\\overrightarrow{SA}_i = \\overrightarrow{OA}_i - \\overrightarrow{OS} = 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i - \\boldsymbol{s}$.\n\n7. Since the vectors $\\boldsymbol{s} - 2\\boldsymbol{a}_i$ all have the same length, all the points $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ lie on a circle $\\Omega$ centered at $S$ with radius $r$.\n\n8. Both circles $\\omega$ (centered at $O$) and $\\Omega$ (centered at $S$) contain the same $n > 2$ points: $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$.\n\n9. The only way two circles can intersect in more than two points is if they coincide entirely. Hence, circles $\\omega$ and $\\Omega$ are identical.\n\n10. Since the circles $\\omega$ and $\\Omega$ are the same, their centers must also coincide. Therefore, $O = S$ or equivalently, the vector $\\boldsymbol{s}$ must be the zero vector:\n    \\[\n    \\boldsymbol{s} = \\mathbf{0}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\boldsymbol{a}_1 + \\boldsymbol{a}_2 + \\ldots + \\boldsymbol{a}_n = \\mathbf{0}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]